00:00:01,915 --> 00:00:03,999
Chapter 96.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,039
The triworks.

3
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:12,238
Besides her hoisted boats, an American whaler is outwardly distinguished by her

4
00:00:12,239 --> 00:00:17,755
triworks. She presents the curious anomaly of the most solid masonry

5
00:00:17,756 --> 00:00:22,719
joining with oak and hemp in constituting the completed ship. It is

6
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:28,399
as if from the open field a brick kiln were transported to her planks.

7
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:33,198
The triworks are planted between the formast and the main mast, the most

8
00:00:33,199 --> 00:00:38,399
roomy part of the deck. The timbers beneath are of a peculiar strength,

9
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:42,954
fitted to sustain the weight of an almost solid mass of brick and mortar,

10
00:00:42,955 --> 00:00:48,634
some 10 ft by 8 square and five in height. The foundation does not

11
00:00:48,635 --> 00:00:53,039
penetrate the deck, but the masonry is firmly secured to the surface by

12
00:00:53,040 --> 00:00:58,399
ponderous knees of iron bracing it on all sides and screwing it down to the

13
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:04,479
timbers. On the flanks, it is cased with wood and at top completely covered by a

14
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:07,435
large sloping battened hatchway.

15
00:01:07,436 --> 00:01:12,795
Removing this hatch we expose the great tripods, two in number and each of

16
00:01:12,796 --> 00:01:15,359
several barrels capacity.

17
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:19,435
When not in use, they are kept remarkably clean. Sometimes they are

18
00:01:19,436 --> 00:01:23,999
polished with soap stone and sand till they shine within like silver punch

19
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,115
bowls. During the night watches, some cynical old sailors will crawl into them

20
